{
  "id": "mitmweb-xsrf-auth-and-header-protections",
  "title": "mitmweb XSRF, auth and header protections",
  "kind": "location",
  "answers": [
    "How does mitmweb protect against XSRF?",
    "Where is mitmweb authentication enforced?",
    "How does the frontend send the XSRF token?"
  ],
  "body": "- `Application.__init__` (mitmproxy/tools/web/app.py) sets `xsrf_cookies=True`, `xsrf_cookie_kwargs=dict(samesite=\"Strict\")`, random `cookie_secret`.\n- Frontend: web/src/js/utils.ts reads the `_xsrf` cookie and sends header `X-XSRFToken` on fetches.\n- Websocket: `WebSocketEventBroadcaster.prepare` touches `self.xsrf_token` so the cookie is set on handshake.\n- `RequestHandler.prepare` returns 403 for non-GET/HEAD/OPTIONS when `Sec-Fetch-Site` is present and not same-origin/none.\n- `RequestHandler.set_default_headers`: X-Frame-Options DENY, CSP default-src 'self', nosniff.\n- Auth: `AuthRequestHandler.__init_subclass__` auto-wraps every HTTP method with `_require_auth` (signed cookie, Bearer token or `token` arg via `is_valid_password`); new handlers inherit this automatically. `IndexHandler.auth_fail` renders login.",
  "tags": [
    "mitmweb",
    "xsrf",
    "auth"
  ],
